// Typical shader look
/*
    #version version_number

    in type in_variable_name;
    out type out_variable_name;
    uniform type uniform_name;

    void main()
    {
    // process input(s) and do some weird graphics stuff
    ...
    // output processed stuff to output variable
    out_variable_name = weird_stuff_we_processed;
    }
*/

// Vertex shader
/*
    When we’re talking specifically about the vertex shader
    each input variable is also known as a vertex attribute.
    There is a maximum number of vertex attributes we’re allowed
    to declare limited by the hardware. OpenGL guarantees there are
    always at least 16 4-component vertex attributes available,
    but some hardware may allow for more which you can retrieve 
    by querying:

    int nrAttributes;
    glGetIntegerv(GL_MAX_VERTEX_ATTRIBS, &nrAttributes);
    std::cout
        << "Maximum nr of vertex attributes supported: "
        << nrAttributes << '\n';


    The GL_MAX_VERTEX_ATTRIBS limit in OpenGL does not directly mean the
    total number of in variables you can declare; rather, it refers to
    the maximum number of 4-component (vec4) slot locations available
    to the vertex shader.
*/

// Types
/*
    GLSL has these types: int, float, double, uint, bool.
    It has also two container types: vectors, matrices

    Vectors, n = 1, 2, 3, or 4.
    vecn:   the default vector of n floats
    bvecn:  a vector of n booleans
    ivecn:  a vector of n integers
    uvecn:  a vector of n unsigned integers
    dvecn:  a vector of n doubles

    To access its components, vec.x, vec.y, vec.z and vec.w

    Swizzling:
    vec2 some_vec;
    vec4 different_vec  = some_vec.xyxx;
    vec3 another_vec    = different_vec.zyw;
    vec4 other_vec      = some_vec.xxxx + another_vec.yxzy;

    Also:
    vec2 vect       = vec2(0.5, 0.8);
    vec4 result     = vec4(vect, 0.0, 0.0);
    vec4 a_result   = vec4(result.xyz, 1.0);
*/

// In and Out
/*
    If we want to send data from one shader to the other we’d have to
    declare an output in the sending shader and a similar input in the
    receiving shader. When the types and the names are equal on both
    sides OpenGL will link those variables together and then it is possible
    to send data between shaders (this is done when linking a program
    object).

    Exception:
    1-) The vertex shader thus requires an extra layout
    specification for its inputs so we can link it with the vertex data.

    2-) the fragment shader requires a vec4 color output variable,
    since the fragment shaders needs to generate a final output color.

*/


// Uniforms
/*
    Uniforms are global, which means:
    1-) You can access them (in your src code) from any
    shader, you do not need to pass them from vertex shader.
    2-) You can set the uniform value and it ll keep its value till it s set again.

    For example in your shader source:
        #version 330 core
        out vec4 FragColor;
        uniform vec4 ourColor; // we set this variable in the OpenGL code.
        void main()
        {
            FragColor = ourColor;
        }

    And in your source code, you can update its value:
        int vertexColorLocation = glGetUniformLocation(shaderProgram, "ourColor");
        glUseProgram(shaderProgram);
        glUniform4f(vertexColorLocation, some_value_1, some_value_2, some_value_3, some_value_4);

    glUniform4f could be glUniform3f and so on:
        f: the function expects a float as its value.
        i: the function expects an int as its value.
        ui: the function expects an unsigned int as its value.
        3f: the function expects 3 floats as its value.
        fv: the function expects a float vector/array as its value.
*/